 ## Proof
 
 **Proof technique:** direct.
 
-1.1 Fix $n\ge1$. In any finite tensor sum, all factor vectors lie in a finite-dimensional subspace $E\subseteq H$. Choose a finite orthonormal basis $(f_j)$ of $E$ by [A4]. Multilinearity expands the sum in the elementary tensors $f_{j_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes f_{j_n}$; the product form makes these tensors orthonormal. Their linear independence follows by applying the multilinear coordinate functionals $(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\mapsto\prod_{r=1}^n\langle x_r,f_{j_r}\rangle$, which descend to the quotient and extract their coefficients. Thus the form is positive definite on every finite tensor span. Its completion is the Hilbert tensor power $H^{\otimes n}$. [A1, A4, construct]
+1.1 Fix $n\ge1$. In any finite tensor sum, all factor vectors lie in a finite-dimensional subspace $E\subseteq H$. Choose a finite orthonormal basis $(f_j)$ of $E$ by [A4]. Multilinearity expands the sum in the elementary tensors $f_{j_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes f_{j_n}$; the product form makes these tensors orthonormal. Their linear independence follows by applying the multilinear coordinate functionals $(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\mapsto\prod_{r=1}^n\langle x_r,f_{j_r}\rangle$, which descend to the quotient and extract their coefficients. Thus the form is positive definite on every finite tensor span. It defines an inner-product space, and [A6] supplies its choice-free sequential Hilbert completion $H^{\otimes n}$. When $n=1$, this construction identifies the tensor power with the already complete space $H$ by the onto assertion in [A6]. [A1, A4, A6, construct]
 
